Andres wrote:

> How difficult would it be to combine multiple different tilesets that may 
> or may not have different resolutions?

Slash'EM's tile system has a number of tools for doing this, if your
tiles are available in the text source code format that is used to ship
the tiles internally. NetHack 4 uses the Slash'EM system with several
improvements, including providing tools to convert tileset images back
into text format so that they can be scaled/edited, etc.. (The tile
manipulation tools are not currently provided in binary builds, although
they will be built if you build from source; if there's enough demand, I
might release binaries for nontechnical tile developers to use.) There's
documentation on how to use them in the NetHack 4 source release.

The 3.4.3 system is somewhat more primitive, and merging tilesets in the
way you suggested would be difficult. It might, however, be possible to
import the tilesets in question into NetHack 4's tile tools, process
them there, and then port them back into 3.4.3. (The import process from
3.4.3 to 4 is relatively well-tested at this point; going the other way
isn't something that I've tried, though, and it's mostly just
coincidence that the tileset tools can handle it at all, as it's not
an operation I normally need to do.)

